  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 300 AM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nA cold front will drape across the Upper Midwest this weekend in \nresponse to the aforementioned northern stream shortwave trough \nzipping into south-central Canada. It and all of its associated cold \nair and rain are forecast to stay north of the Mid-Mississippi \nValley, so our warm streak will continue through the weekend. High \nclouds blowing off from showers to our north and west may stunt \nwarming Saturday and Sunday afternoons, but highs are still expected \nto climb well into the 70s each day.\n\nThe mid-level ridge centered over the eastern 2/3rds of the CONUS \nwill deamplify early next week as a longwave trough over the Desert \nSouthwest finally pushes into the Plains and weakens into a \nshortwave. Its progression is expected to shove the cold front into \nthe Mid-Mississippi Valley sometime around Tuesday, but the \nvariability in guidance is too high to pinpoint an exact time. This \nstems from differences in the phasing, amplitude, track, and timing \nof the shortwave. For instance, the ENS shows a stronger shortwave \nthat swings more poleward than the other ensembles. As a result, the \nfront barely clears Chicago, and the majority of our CWA has a 40-\n80% chance of reaching 70 degrees by 1pm Tuesday. The GEPS, on the \nother hand, shows a very weak and much more southerly shortwave. In \nthis solution, the front clears St. Louis and the CWA has a 20-50% \nchance of reaching 70 degrees by 1pm Tuesday. Then there is the \nquestion of whether the front actually passes and we cool down, or \nif it lifts northward as a warm front and the unseasonable warmth \ncontinues further. This is a question that can't be answered right \nnow given the reliability of the data, but it is what we'll be \nwatching for the next several days.",
